// Branch coverage for tls: the package's own tests, and nothing else.
//
//   deno task coverage:tls
//   deno task coverage:tls --verbose
//
// The seventh of the packages `issues/system/0205` records as unmeasured, and the largest so far at
// 4,195 lines of source against 185 tests. It is also the one with the strongest oracle in the
// repository: `interop_test.wac`, `rsa_interop_test.wac` and `client_test.wac` speak to a real
// OpenSSL over a real socket, and `x509_path_test.wac` checks certificate path building against it.
//
// **A hundred of the 185 tests expect a trap**, which is the highest proportion of any package
// measured. They go through `trapCase` below rather than `main`: a trap in `main` ends the run, and
// the report then looks exactly like one taken before any of them were wired in — the failure mode
// that makes a coverage number quietly describe a third of the tests it claims to include.
//
// What to expect from the reading. Every package measured so far has had its gap in the refusals,
// because its corpus was produced by something that works. This package's oracle is an implementation
// that also *refuses* — OpenSSL rejects a malformed record, an expired certificate, a downgraded
// suite — so the interop tests do drive refusal paths. Whether that changes the shape of the gap is
// the question worth asking of the first number rather than answering in advance.

